<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec_rc-discharge" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
	<title>Discharging a Capacitor</title>
	
		<activity>
      	<title>Warm-up</title>
	  	<introduction>
      			<p></p>
				<p>The circuit below contains a capacitor, an open switch, and a light bulb.  The capacitor has known capacitance <m>C_1</m>, charge <m>+Q</m> on the top plate, and charge <m>-Q</m> on the bottom plate, as shown in the figure.</p>
					
				<figure xml:id="RC0">
			  	<title>Capacitor Discharge Figure</title>
				<caption>A circuit diagram with both a light bulb and a capacitor.</caption>
				<image source="RC-d.png" width = "50%" ><description>A circuit diagram with a charged capacitor on the left (positive on top), an open switch, and a light bulb on the right.</description></image>
				</figure>
	  	</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Use the definition of <xref ref="def_capacitance"/> to write an equation for the voltage across the capacitor.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<answer>
				<p>
					See the definition of the <xref ref="a_capacitance"/>.
				</p>
			</answer>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Suppose the switch is closed at <m>t = 0</m>.  For an instant immediately after the switch is closed, sketch a voltage diagram for the circuit.  How does the voltage across the light bulb compare to the voltage across the capacitor?
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Give a qualitative description of what you think will happen to the following two quantities as time moves forward: (1) the charge on the capacitor and (2) the brightness of the light bulb.  Support your answers with a mechanistic explanation.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Sketch a graph predicting the <em>current</em> through the circuit as a function of time, starting at <m>t = 0</m>.  Comment on the value of the current in the limit as <m>t \rightarrow \infty</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
		</activity>
					
		

				
	<corollary xml:id="vid-discharging-capacitor">
	<title>Discharging Capacitor</title>
	
	<statement>
		<p>
			
		</p>

		<video youtube="EuU7Me6dvzg"><description>Analyzing a discharging capacitor.</description></video>

	</statement>
  </corollary>
  

  <subsubsection><title>Key Ideas</title></subsubsection>
	
	<p>When a capacitor with initial charge <m>Q_0</m> and capacitance <m>C</m> is connected to a resistor with resistance <m>R</m>, the charge on the capacitor as a function of time is given by <me>Q(t) = Q_0 e^{-t/RC}</me></p>
			
  		<subsubsection>
		<title>Activities</title>
		<activity>
      <title>Capacitor Behavior</title>
      <p></p>
			<p>Provide a physical argument to justify the generalized statement below about capacitors.</p>
			<assumption xml:id="a_capacitor-continuity">
		<title>Capacitor Continuity</title>
		<statement>
			<p>
				
			</p>
			<p>
				The voltage across an ideal capacitor in a circuit should be continuous, but the current through an ideal capacitor does not have to be continuous.
			</p>
			
		</statement>
	</assumption>
		</activity>
		<activity>
      <title>Exploring RC Circuits</title>
	  <introduction>
		<p>
			
		</p>
		<p>
			Consider a discharging capacitor for the following questions.
		</p>
	  </introduction>
	  <task>
		<statement>
			<p>Sketch a graph of the voltage across the discharging capacitor as a function of time.</p>
		</statement>
	  </task>
	  <task>
			<p>Determine the units of the quantity <m>RC</m>.  Describe how the graph you made would change if you were to <em>increase</em> this quantity.  Use your answers to give a brief physical interpretation for it (that is, what <em>kind</em> of quantity is it, and what is it trying to tell you about the behavior of the circuit?).</p>
	  </task>
		<task>
			<p>Calculate an expression for the current through the resistor as a function of time.</p>
		</task>
		<task>
			<p>Sketch a graph of the current through the resistor as a function of time.  Make sense of this graph given your answers to the previous activities.</p>
		</task>
		</activity>
	</subsubsection>

<!--NO AI-->
</section>

